module bindings.javascript.sandbox_function_introspection_property_gap;

// A sandbox that interprets author-supplied code has to decide, for every
// property read its interpreter performs on a live host value, which names
// are safe to hand back. The JavaScript specification makes one part of that
// decision non-optional: `caller`, `callee` and `arguments` are the
// introspection properties (Function.prototype.caller,
// Function.prototype.arguments, arguments.callee) whose native getters
// disclose the function that is running -- and inside a sandbox the function
// that is running is the interpreter's own runtime callback, a host function
// holding host privileges. Strict mode and CSP disable them for exactly this
// reason, and every documented JS sandbox denies them by name.
//
// The interpreter read path shows its name denylist in the guards around the
// read: it compares the computed key against the prototype-machinery names
// (`prototype`, `__proto__`) and throws the sandbox's own access-denial
// signal. A guard set that covers those and never names the introspection
// trio forwards the callback itself to sandboxed code -- `(function fn() {
// return fn.caller; })()` recovers it, and invoking it directly with a
// crafted interpreter argument object performs reads and calls outside every
// guard.
//
// Every fact is library documentation, not incident vocabulary: the
// access-denial signal is the library's exported error class, the two
// prototype names are the guard the read path already documents, and the
// trio is the specification's own list. The omission clause matches names
// exactly (`in`, not `contains`) so a name list, a Set lookup and an explicit
// comparison satisfy it equally -- the discriminator is which names the guard
// covers, not the spelling a particular fix chose.
//
// Module scope rather than function scope, because the denied names may be a
// module-level Set or constant whose consuming membership test is a two-line
// check inside the op: a function-scope query sees the test and none of the
// names. All three clauses read AST-derived tokens (call sites and string
// literals), so the module's comments cannot satisfy the anchor or silence
// the omission -- prose naming `caller` is not a guard. The residual runs in
// the false-negative direction only: a module that spells one of the three
// names in a quoted literal for an unrelated reason is not reported.
binding sandboxFunctionIntrospectionPropertyNotDenied {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.call in ["SandboxAccessError"]
    and node.context.literal in ["prototype"]
    and node.context.literal in ["__proto__"]
    and not (node.context.literal in ["caller", "callee", "arguments"])
  emit issue code.SandboxHostObjectMemberDenylistGap at node
}
